%[text] %[text:anchor:T_9634033F] # Derivative Rules for Transcendental Functions
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] For some common functions, applying the limit definition of the derivative requires more complicated limit proofs than those required for polynomials. This script presents the derivatives of sines, cosines, exponentials and natural logarithms through numerical approximation.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Startup Instructions**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:7d04) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Bright idea symbol](text:image:23ce) If you find the MATLAB coding in this script to be challenging, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2 hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:9e9f)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A488789D] ## What is a Transcendental Function?
%[text] Have you encountered transcendental numbers like $e\\approx 2.71828182459046${"altText":"e approximately 2.71828182459046"} or $\\pi \\approx 3.141592653589793${"altText":"pi approximately 3.141592653589793"}? They are called *transcendental* because they are not *algebraic,* that is they are not a root of any finite degree polynomial equation with integer coefficients. All rational numbers are algebraic, e.g., $x = \\frac{p}{q}${"altText":"x = (p)\/(q)"} is a root of $y = qx-p${"altText":"y = qx-p"}, but many irrational numbers are algebraic as well, e.g., $x=\\sqrt{5}${"altText":"x=sqrt(5)"} is a root of $y=x^2-5${"altText":"y=x^2-5"}. Despite the lack of a related finite degree polynomial equation, you can still approximate transcendental numbers by their sequence of decimal expansions, e.g., 3, 3.1, 3.14, 3.141, 3.1415, 3.14159, etc. A *transcendental function* is a function that you can approximate by a sequence of polynomial functions, but that is not the solution to a polynomial equation itself. The most common transcendental functions are the exponential, logarithm, and trigonometric functions.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_12495CEA] ## Numerical Approximation
%[text] Recall the graphical approach to understanding the limit definition of the derivative as [approximation by secant lines](file:./DefinitionSoln.m) approaching a tangent line. This script will build on that same idea by identifying adjacent points separated by a small step size on a curve and evaluating the slope of the secant line through those points. As the step size gets smaller, the slope of the secant line will numerically approximate the slope of the tangent line. There are entire courses and research programs devoted to numerical analysis that ask and answer questions about how best to describe and compute these approximations, but that is beyond the scope of this script.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3F24D48C] ## Trigonometric Functions
%[text] Trigonometric functions are the family of functions that relate the ratios of sides to the angles in right triangles. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A right triangle with one acute angle theta. The length of the leg opposite angle theta is "opp", the length of the leg adjacent to angle theta is "adj" and the length of the hypotenuse is "hyp".](text:image:42e6)
%[text] The abbreviations are
%[text]{"align":"center"}  opp = "length of the side opposite the angle $\\theta${"altText":"theta"}",                                  
%[text]{"align":"center"} adj = "length of the side between the right angle and the angle $\\theta${"altText":"theta"}", 
%[text] and 
%[text]{"align":"center"} hyp = "length of the hypotenuse".                                                      
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C67E465E] ### Sines & Cosines
%[text] The function $y = \\sin(\\theta)${"altText":"y = sin(theta)"} tracks the ratio $\\frac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{hyp}}${"altText":"(opp)\/(hyp)"}. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A graph of sine of theta between theta equals minus pi and theta equals 3 pi](text:image:3908)
%[text] The function $y=\\cos(\\theta)${"altText":"y=cos(theta)"} tracks the ratio $\\frac{\\text{adj}}{\\text{hyp}}${"altText":"(adj)\/(hyp)"}. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A graph of cosine of theta from theta equals minus pi to theta equals 3 pi](text:image:07c9)
%[text] By placing a right triangle into a unit circle, the intimate relationship between sines and cosines becomes clear.
%[text] This is shown in the file [UnitCircleAnimation.mp4](<matlab: OpenOnComputer("UnitCircleAnimation.mp4")>) or by generating the animation below: 
  %[control:button:21a1]{"position":[1,2]}
UnitCircleAnimation();
%[text] ![](text:image:47cb) **Pro-tip**. If this script is running too slowly, you can click the **Stop** button in the **Live Editor** tab. That will terminate the script.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2E23C42F] ### Derivative of Sine
%[text] ![](text:image:8c92) **Activity.** Let's try to determine the derivative $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}${"altText":"(dy)\/(dtheta)"} of $y=\\sin(\\theta)${"altText":"y=sin(theta)"} by computing its numerical approximation. First, let's pick a step size $h${"altText":"h"} to use in the difference quotient: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta} \\approx \\frac{y(\\theta+h)-y(\\theta)}{h}${"altText":"(dy)\/(dtheta) approximately (y(theta+h)-y(theta))\/(h)"},
%[text] and graph the result:
h = 0.2;                % User-defined step size set to disallow h = 0, default 0.2 %[control:slider:9760]{"position":[5,8]}
                       % Run this section %[control:button:3370]{"position":[1,2]}
if ~exist("sinTHandle","var") || ~isvalid(sinTHandle)
    figure                                      % Initialize a figure
    [sinsHandle,sinmptHandle,sinTHandle] = GraphApproxDer(@(t)sin(t),"sin",h,[-pi 3*pi]);
    % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
else
    UpdateDerApprox(@(t)sin(t),"sin",h,[-pi 3*pi],sinsHandle,sinmptHandle,sinTHandle)
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7ecc) **Try**. Make the value of the step size larger and smaller and observe the impact on the approximate derivative of $\\sin(\\theta)${"altText":"sin(theta)"}that is shown in the graph above.
%[text] ![](text:image:85c4) **Exercise 1**. Consider the approximate derivative $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\left\[\\sin(\\theta)\\right\]${"altText":"(d)\/(dtheta)\[sin(theta)\]"} that is graphed above.
%[text] 1. Could the derivative be a polynomial function? Why or why not? \
poly = "no";         % User-defined answer to Question 1.1 %[control:dropdown:2f77]{"position":[8,12]}
% If the answer has changed from the default, "I don't know", display
% appropriate feedback.
if poly == "yes"
    disp("All polynomials p(t) diverge towards positive or negative infinity as p goes to infinity.")
    disp("That is not true of this derivative. It is not a polynomial.")
elseif poly == "no"
    disp("That is correct.")
    disp("This cannot be a polynomial because it does not have the correct limiting behavior.")
end
%%
%[text]          2\. Does the derivative have any asymptotes? Why or why not? If there are any asymptotes, what are they?
asymptotes = "no";   % User-defined answer to whether there are any asymptotes and of which type %[control:dropdown:1aef]{"position":[14,18]}
yasymp = 0.5;       % If there is a horizontal asymptote, the user should set the value here %[control:editfield:92a5]{"position":[10,13]}
xasymp = 0.5;       % If there is a vertical asymptote, the user should set the value here %[control:editfield:2b89]{"position":[10,13]}
% In this case there are no asymptotes, but for symmetry with later
% questions the option to define them is included here as well
% If the answer has changed from the default, "I don't know", display
% appropriate feedback.
if (asymptotes == "yHoriz") || (yasymp ~= 0.5)
    disp("No, there is not a horizontal asymptote. Please try again.")
elseif (asymptotes == "yVert") || (xasymp ~= 0.5)
    disp("No, there is not a vertical asymptote. Please try again.")
elseif asymptotes == "no"
    disp("Correct. The derivative does not have any asymptotes.")
end
%%
%[text]          3\. Is the derivative periodic? Why or why not? If it is periodic, what is the period and what is the peak amplitude?
periodic = 1;   % User-defined answer to whether the derivative is periodic %[control:dropdown:8bc8]{"position":[12,13]}
% Interact with the user based on their choices, with specific messages
% tagged to the default options which have been chosen to be incorrect
if periodic == 1
    disp("Yes, this derivative is periodic. Please set the correct period and amplitude:")
    period = 2*pi;          amplitude = 1; %[control:editfield:15c1]{"position":[14,18]} %[control:editfield:936c]{"position":[41,42]}
    if period == 2*pi
        disp("The period is correct.")
    elseif abs(period-2*pi) < .0033    % Check for an answer based on 3.14 rather than pi
        disp("Please use the exact value pi rather than the approximation 3.14.")
    elseif period == pi
        disp("The period is not pi.")
        disp("Dashed blue lines show the proposed period.")
    else
        disp("The period is incorrect. Please try again.")
        disp("Dashed blue lines show the proposed period.")
    end
    if amplitude == 1
        disp("The amplitude is 1, that is correct.")
    elseif abs(amplitude-1) < .01       % Prompt the user to find the exact value
        disp("Please be more precise. The amplitude is an integer.")
    elseif amplitude == 1/2
        disp("The amplitude is not 1/2.")
        disp("Dotted red lines show the proposed amplitude.")
    else
        disp("The amplitude is incorrect. Please try again.")
        disp("Dotted red lines show the proposed amplitude.")
    end
    if ~exist("h","var")
        h = .2                                     % Set the default step size if not user-defined
    end
    GraphApproxDer(@(t)sin(t),"sin",h,[-pi 3*pi]); % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
    if abs(amplitude)>1                   % Resize the plot to show the proposed amplitude line
        ylim([-1.1 1.1*abs(amplitude)]);
    end
    hold on
    xline(0,"--",SeriesIndex=1)           % Plot the proposed period at x=0 and x=period, dashed blue line
    xline(period,"--",SeriesIndex=1)
    yline(0,":",SeriesIndex=2)            % Plot the proposed amplitude at y=0 and y=amplitude, dotted red line
    yline(amplitude,":",SeriesIndex=2)
    hold off
elseif periodic == 0                      % If solution is incorrect, offer a hint
    disp("No, that is incorrect. Does this function ever repeat?")
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:11f0) **Exercise 2**. Taking into account the results of Exercise 1, the derivative $y'(\\theta)${"altText":"y'(theta)"} should be a function that you recognize. Use the independent variable name `t for` $\\theta${"altText":"theta"}, what is the derivative of `sin(t)` with respect to `t`?
derGuess = @(t)cos(t);  % User-defined anonymous symbolic function, default @(t)t %[control:editfield:4f26]{"position":[16,22]}
                            % Run this section %[control:button:8c3c]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckSinDer(derGuess)                 % CheckSinDer is defined in Helper Functions
% CheckSinDer takes the function handle derGuess and checks whether it is equivalent
% to the derivative of sin(t)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C1624E8D] ### Derivative of Cosine
%[text] ![](text:image:2b45) **Activity**. Try to determine the derivative $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}${"altText":"(dy)\/(dtheta)"} of $y=\\cos(\\theta)${"altText":"y=cos(theta)"} by computing its numerical approximation just as you did for $\\sin(\\theta)${"altText":"sin(theta)"} up above. First, pick a step size $h${"altText":"h"} to use in the difference quotient, $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta} \\approx \\frac{y(\\theta+h)-y(\\theta)}{h}${"altText":"(dy)\/(dtheta) approximately (y(theta+h)-y(theta))\/(h)"}, and graph the result again.
h = 0.2;               % User-defined step size, default h = 0.2 %[control:slider:1074]{"position":[5,8]}
                           % Run this section %[control:button:1b1d]{"position":[1,2]}
if ~exist("cosTHandle","var") || ~isvalid(cosTHandle)
    figure                                     % Initialize a figure
    [cossHandle,cosmptHandle,cosTHandle] = GraphApproxDer(@(t)cos(t),"cos",h,[-pi 3*pi]);
    % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
else
    UpdateDerApprox(@(t)cos(t),"cos",h,[-pi 3*pi],cossHandle,cosmptHandle,cosTHandle)
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8BC33946] Remember that the derivative of $y=\\sin(\\theta)${"altText":"y=sin(theta)"} was $y' = \\sin\\left(\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)${"altText":"y' = sin(theta+(pi)\/(2))"}. It appears that the derivative of $y=\\cos(\\theta)${"altText":"y=cos(theta)"} is also a sinusoidal function with the same amplitude. Use the slider to test values of the phase shift $\\phi${"altText":"phi"} to determine the function $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\left\[\\cos(\\theta)\\right\] = \\cos(\\theta+\\phi)${"altText":"(d)\/(dtheta)\[cos(theta)\] = cos(theta+phi)"}. You want the solid blue line to pass through the square points.
% User-defined multiplier, default mult = 0
mult = 0.5; %[control:slider:87a6]{"position":[8,11]}
if ~exist("h","var")
    h = .2                                      % If not user-defined, set default h = 0.2
end
figure                                          % Initialize a figure
GraphApproxDer(@(t)cos(t),"cos",h,[-pi 3*pi]);  % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
% GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
%      derivative with step size h
hold on
GraphDerGuess(@(t)cos(t),mult)                  % GraphDerGuess is defined in Helper Functions
% GraphDerGuess plots the function given by fh with a phase shift of mult*pi
%      and adds annotation noting that \phi = mult*pi on the plot
%      in the lower left hand quadrant of the plot
hold off
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F3F0C2C4] Once you have chosen the best possible function to fit the approximate derivative, you can check your result here using `t` as your independent variable. If you wish, you may also use `phi` for the value $\\phi${"altText":"phi"} you set above or `pi` for the value $\\pi${"altText":"pi"} :
phi = mult*pi;                                 % User-defined multiplier based on the slider mult above
cosDerGuess = @(t)cos(t+phi);        % User-defined anonymous function, default @(t) t*phi*pi %[control:editfield:30e2]{"position":[19,29]}
                                     % Run this section %[control:button:8419]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckCosDer(cosDerGuess)                       % CheckCosDer is defined in Helper Functions
% CheckCosDer compares the derivative of cos(t) to fh(t) and prints a message
%      of success or failure using disp()
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E4A003B3] ![](text:image:8f69) **Try** 
%[text] What happens if you look at the derivative of $y=\\sin(c\\theta)${"altText":"y=sin(ctheta)"} for some constant value $c${"altText":"c"}?
h = 0.021;               % User-defined step size, default h = 0.021 %[control:slider:192c]{"position":[5,10]}
c = 3;                       % User-defined constant (numerical) value, default c=3 %[control:editfield:3bf8]{"position":[5,6]}
if abs(c)>1
    bds = [-pi 3*pi];
else
    bds = [-pi abs(3/c)*pi];
end
                           % Run this section %[control:button:7d12]{"position":[1,2]}
if ~exist("sincTHandle","var")||~isvalid(sincTHandle)
    figure                                      % Initialize a figure
    [sincHandle,sincmptHandle,sincTHandle,sincFH] = GraphApproxDer(@(t)sin(c*t),"sin",h,bds);
    % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
    sincTHandle.String = "$y = \sin(c\theta)$ with $c = $" + c;
    legend("$\sin(c\theta)$","Selected Points","Difference Quotient","AutoUpdate","off")
    ax = sincTHandle.Parent;
    if abs(1.1*c)>1.1
        ax.YLim = [-abs(1.1*c) abs(1.1*c)];
    else
        ax.YLim = [-1.1 1.1];
    end
else
    UpdateDerApprox(@(t)sin(c*t),"sin",h,bds,sincHandle,sincmptHandle,sincTHandle)
    if c == round(c,0)
        sincStr = compose("$y = \\sin(%.0f\\theta)$",c);
    elseif c == round(c,1)
        sincStr = compose("$y = \\sin(%.1f\\theta)$",c);
    elseif c == round(c,2)
        sincStr = compose("$y = \\sin(%.2f\\theta)$",c);
    else
        sincStr = "$y = \sin(c\theta)$ with $c = $" + c;
    end
    sincTHandle.String = sincStr;
    sincFH.Function = sin(c*t);
    if abs(1.1*c)>1.1
        ax.YLim = [-abs(1.1*c) abs(1.1*c)];
        ax.XLim = [-pi 3*pi];
    else
        ax.YLim = [-1.1 1.1];
        ax.XLim = [-pi abs(3/c)*pi];
    end
end
%[text] ![](text:image:0a62) **Reflect**
%[text] 1. What is the derivative $y'(\\theta)${"altText":"y'(theta)"} for the function $y = \\sin(7\\theta)${"altText":"y = sin(7theta)"}? What is the derivative $y'(\\theta)${"altText":"y'(theta)"} for the function $y=\\sin(-.36\\theta)${"altText":"y=sin(-.36theta)"}?
%[text] 2. What do you think is the derivative $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}${"altText":"(dy)\/(dtheta)"} for the function $y = \\cos(c\\theta)${"altText":"y = cos(ctheta)"} for some constant value $c${"altText":"c"}? Why?
%[text] 3. What do you think is the derivative $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\left\[\\sin(\\theta + \\phi)\\right\]${"altText":"(d)\/(dtheta)\[sin(theta + phi)\]"} for some constant value $\\phi${"altText":"phi"}? Why? \
