%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7B0D2DF3] ### ![](text:image:4f77) Application: Springs
%[text] Consider a simple mass-spring system with mass $m${"altText":"m"} and stiffness coefficient $k\>0${"altText":"k\>0"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A yellow-orange square labelled "Mass m" is connected hoizontally to a wall by a spring with spring constant k](text:image:3076)
%[text] Then the motion of the mass as a function of the horizontal displacement $x = p(t)${"altText":"x = p(t)"} is given by a differential equation (that is an equation involving derivatives): 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\text{Force} = -k\\cdot \\left(\\text{Displacement from Equilibrium}\\right)${"altText":"Force = -k \* (Displacement from Equilibrium)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Mass} \\times \\text{Acceleration} = -k\\cdot p(t)${"altText":"Mass \* Acceleration = -k \* p(t)"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m\\cdot \\frac{d}{dt}\\left\[\\frac{d}{dt}\\left\[p(t)\\right\]\\right\] = -k\\cdot p(t)${"altText":"m \* (d)\/(dt)\[(d)\/(dt)\[p(t)\]\] = -k \* p(t)"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = -\\frac{k}{m}p(t)${"altText":"(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = -(k)\/(m)p(t)"},
%[text] where the force generated by the spring is acting in opposition to the displacement from equilibrium.  Thus, if $p(t)\>0${"altText":"p(t)\>0"}, then the acceleration of the mass is in the negative direction towards zero and if $p(t)\<0${"altText":"p(t)\<0"}, then the acceleration of the mass is in the positive direction towards zero. The equilibrium position, where the mass will stay at rest if it starts at rest is $p(t)=0${"altText":"p(t)=0"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:5f04) **Exercise 4**. Let's consider a case where $k=m=1${"altText":"k=m=1"}. This simplifies the differential equation to $\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = -p(t)${"altText":"(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = -p(t)"}. Check whether any functions that you know satisfy this differential equation, that is if you calculated the second derivative $p''(t)${"altText":"p''(t)"} that is the same function as $p(t)${"altText":"p(t)"} originally multiplied by -1.
%[text] 1. $p(t) = x^n${"altText":"p(t) = x^n"} for any constant $n${"altText":"n"}
%[text] 2. $p(t) = \\sin(t)${"altText":"p(t) = sin(t)"}
%[text] 3. $p(t) = \\cos(t)${"altText":"p(t) = cos(t)"}
%[text] 4. $p(t) = 3\\sin(t)-7\\cos(t)${"altText":"p(t) = 3sin(t)-7cos(t)"} \
%[text] Select the corresponding check box if $p''(t) = -p(t)${"altText":"p''(t) = -p(t)"} and leave unselected if $p''(t)\\neq -p(t)${"altText":"p''(t) != -p(t)"}.
% User-selected booleans to identify which statements are true or false,
% corresponding to Exercise 4, Statements 1-4.
one = false; two = true; three = true; four = true; %[control:checkbox:8a7f]{"position":[7,12]} %[control:checkbox:3202]{"position":[20,24]} %[control:checkbox:910b]{"position":[34,38]} %[control:checkbox:0ada]{"position":[47,51]}
CheckEqns(one,two,three,four);  % CheckEqns is defined in Helper functions
% CheckEqns checks the truth of one, two, three, and four and prints feedback
                 % Run this section %[control:button:1e22]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_69B1371F] ![](text:image:9a27) **Reflect**
%[text] 1. Why are there multiple different functions that all solve the same differential equation?
%[text] 2. Can you sketch a solution to a differential equation even if you don't know how to write the solution as a function? Why or why not?
%[text] 3. Can you find a solution to the differential equation when $k${"altText":"k"} and $m${"altText":"m"} are not both 1? For instance, what about $\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = -4p${"altText":"(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = -4p"}?  \
