%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F4F79120] ## Exponential Functions
%[text] The full beauty of exponential functions only starts to come into focus when you consider them in the realm of calculus. 
%[text] ![](text:image:11b4) **Pro-tip**. In MATLAB, use the command `exp(2*x)` for $e^{2x}${"altText":"e^(2x)"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A graph of the exponential function of x from x equals negative 3 to x equals 4](text:image:7c17)
%[text] ![](text:image:5bb7) **Demonstration**. Take the same approach you did with $y=\\cos(\\theta)${"altText":"y=cos(theta)"} and compute and graph some values of the difference quotient for $f(t) = e^t${"altText":"f(t) = e^t"},
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Difference Quotient} = \\frac{e^{t+h}-e^{t}}{h} = e^t\\left(\\frac{e^h-1}{h}\\right)${"altText":"Difference Quotient = (e^(t+h)-e^(t))\/(h) = e^t((e^h-1)\/(h))"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
h = 0.2;          % User-defined step size, default h = 0.2 %[control:slider:933d]{"position":[5,8]}
                      % Run this section %[control:button:3488]{"position":[1,2]}
if ~exist("expTHandle","var") || ~isvalid(expTHandle)
    figure                                % Initialize a figure
    [expsHandle,expmptHandle,expTHandle] = GraphApproxDer(@(t)exp(t),"exp",h,[-3 4]);
    % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
else
    UpdateDerApprox(@(t)exp(t),"exp",h,[-3 4],expsHandle,expmptHandle,expTHandle)
end
%[text] ![](text:image:1977) **Try** Set some different values of `h` using the slider. Then check your hypothesis. Remember to use `t` as the independent variable.
%%
myGuess = @(t) exp(t);   % User-defined prediction of the derivative of exp(t) %[control:editfield:9cd0]{"position":[15,22]}
                            % Run this section %[control:button:4454]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckExpDer(myGuess);                 % CheckExpDer is defined in Helper Functions
% CheckExpDer compares myGuess to the derivative of exp(t)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F40BE6FD] ### ![](text:image:9850) **Application: Population Growth**
%[text] Say there is a population of bunnies like these:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_0DFCEDBC]  ![A collection of large and small bunnies](text:image:2fca) 
%[text] If the number of bunnies at time $t${"altText":"t"} in years is modelled by the function $B(t)${"altText":"B(t)"}, then the rate of change of the number of bunnies over time is given by the derivative $\\frac{dB}{dt}${"altText":"(dB)\/(dt)"} with units of bunnies per year. Say at time $0${"altText":"0"} the bunny population is $100${"altText":"100"}, what is happening to the bunny population over time if $\\frac{dB}{dt} = B(t)${"altText":"(dB)\/(dt) = B(t)"}?
%[text] ![](text:image:571d) **Reflect**
%[text] 1. What type of function is its own derivative? How can you set the initial value to 100?
%[text] 2. What does this imply about the bunny population over the long term? 
%[text] 3. In what ways is this model realistic? In what ways is it unrealistic?  \
%[text] ![](text:image:66d5) **Activity**. The difference quotient approximation to the derivative can be used to approximate the solution to a differential equation with a known initial value. Say there are 100 bunnies to start, so $B(0)=100${"altText":"B(0)=100"}. Each year, assume all the bunnies reproduce once. Assuming that the bunny population is half male and half female and that the average litter size is $k${"altText":"k"}, that means that the number of new baby bunnies is given by $\\frac{dB}{dt} = k\\left(\\frac{B(t)}{2}\\right)${"altText":"(dB)\/(dt) = k((B(t))\/(2))"}, so 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{B(t+h)-B(t)}{h} \\approx k\\left(\\frac{B(t)}{2}\\right)${"altText":"(B(t+h)-B(t))\/(h) approximately k((B(t))\/(2))"}. 
%[text] Thus, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B(t+h) = B(t) + hk\\left(\\frac{B(t)}{2}\\right)${"altText":"B(t+h) = B(t) + hk((B(t))\/(2))"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:803e) **Try**. The following code implements the model $B(t+h) = B(t)+k\\left(\\frac{B(t)}{2}\\right)${"altText":"B(t+h) = B(t)+k((B(t))\/(2))"} with $h=1${"altText":"h=1"} year for `tEnd` years, using the new bunny population each time. Therefore, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B(1) = B(0+1) = B(0)+k\\left(\\frac{B(0)}{2}\\right) = 100 + 50k${"altText":"B(1) = B(0+1) = B(0)+k((B(0))\/(2)) = 100 + 50k"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B(2) = B(1+1) = B(1)+k\\left(\\frac{B(1)}{2}\\right) = 100+50k + k\\left(50+25k\\right) = 100+100k+25k^2${"altText":"B(2) = B(1+1) = B(1)+k((B(1))\/(2)) = 100+50k + k(50+25k) = 100+100k+25k^2"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vdots${"altText":"..."} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B(\\text{tEnd}) = B(\\text{tEnd}-1)+k\\left(\\frac{B(\\text{tEnd}-1)}{2}\\right)${"altText":"B(tEnd) = B(tEnd-1)+k((B(tEnd-1))\/(2))"} 
%[text] The code looks like this:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] Bdata(1) = 100;                                % Set B(0)=100
%[text] for j = 1:tEnd                                 % Repeat tEnd times
%[text]     Bdata(j+1) = Bdata(j) + k*(Bdata(j)/2);    % Update the population at each timestep
%[text] end
%[text] ```
%[text] Run this code with different choices for litter size `k` and the total number of years, `tEnd,` and compare the number of bunnies to the reference function $y(t) = 100e^t${"altText":"y(t) = 100e^t"}, which starts at the same value and grows exponentially. How do your results change with different values for `k`? For `tEnd`?
k = 8;        % User-defined litter size, default k = 5 %[control:slider:99fb]{"position":[5,6]}
tEnd = 5;     % User-defined year tEnd, default tEnd = 5 %[control:slider:79b0]{"position":[8,9]}
                      % Run this section %[control:button:4d54]{"position":[1,2]}
tdata = 0:tEnd;             % Set up a time vector
Bdata = size(tdata);        % Initialize a vector to use for the bunny population
Bdata(1) = 100;             % Set B(0)=100
for j = 1:tEnd
    Bdata(j+1) = Bdata(j) + k*(Bdata(j)/2);    % Update the population at each timestep
end
figure                           % Initialize a figure
plot(tdata,Bdata,"*-")           % Plot the calculated bunny population versus time
hold on
plot(tdata,100*exp(tdata),"*-")  % Add the plot of 100*e^t versus time
hold off
% Add a title, labels, and a legend to the plot
title("Modelling the Bunny Population")
xlabel("t (years)")
ylabel("# of Bunnies")
legend("Bunny Model with k = " + k, "y = 100e^t","Location","northwest")
%[text] ![](text:image:08ec) **Reflect**.
%[text] 1. Why are the two functions different?
%[text] 2. Which function is growing faster? Why?
%[text] 3. Can you find a parameter `k` where the Bunny Model is growing slower than $100e^t${"altText":"100e^t"}? Can you find a parameter `k` where the Bunny Model is growing faster than $100e^t${"altText":"100e^t"}?  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FB72ADB4] #### Refining the Population Model
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7D8BAE22] As you can see, the first Bunny Population Model is very coarse, resulting in piecewise defined graphs that do not look like smooth curves because you are only checking on the bunny population once each year. In fact, rabbits mature very quickly and can have several litters of babies each year, so let's allow a bit more flexibility with the time step with the same `tEnd` as above:
if ~exist("k","var")            % Set up the default parameters
    k = 5;                      % in case the previous section hasn't run, default k = 5
end
if ~exist("tend","var")         % Set up the default parameters
    tEnd = 5;                   % in case the previous section hasn't run, default tEnd = 5
end
dt = 0.25; % User-defined time step between litters (in years), default dt = 0.25 %[control:slider:8526]{"position":[6,10]}
  %[control:button:17bc]{"position":[1,2]}
tData = 0:dt:tEnd;              % Set up a time vector with step size dt
BunnyData = size(tData);        % Initialize a vector for the bunny population
BunnyData(1) = 100;             % Set B(0)=100
for j = 1:length(tData)-1
    BunnyData(j+1) = BunnyData(j)+dt*(k/2)*BunnyData(j);   % Update the population at each timestep
end
figure                          % Initialize a figure
plot(tData,BunnyData)           % Plot the calculated bunny population versus time
hold on
syms t
fplot(100*exp(t),[0 tEnd])      % Add a plot of 100*e^t versus time
hold off
title("Modelling the Bunny Population")
xlabel("t (years)")
ylabel("# of Bunnies")
if exist("Bdata","var") && exist("tdata","var") % Check if the basic bunny model has already been run
    hold on
    scatter(tdata,Bdata,"filled")               % Add a scatter plot of the bunny population with dt=1
    legend("Numerical Model with k = " + k + " and step size " + dt,"y = 100e^t","Numerical " + ...
        "Model with k = " + k + " and step size 1","Location","northwest")
    hold off
else                    % Remind the user to run the previous section and label only the current plots
    disp("Run the Bunny Model before this section to compare the two.")
    legend("Numerical Model with k = " + k + " and step size " + dt,"y = 100e^t","Location","northwest")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:0374) **Reflect**
%[text] 1. What is the impact on the model as the step size between generations approaches 0? Is it realistic to allow the step size to approach 0 here? Why or why not?
%[text] 2. What type of function solves the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = ky${"altText":"(dy)\/(dx) = ky"} for $k${"altText":"k"} a constant? Does it matter what $k${"altText":"k"} is?
%[text] 3. What type of function solves the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = kx${"altText":"(dy)\/(dx) = kx"} for $k${"altText":"k"} a constant? Does it matter what $k${"altText":"k"} is?
%[text] 4. What is the derivative $\\frac{d}{dt}\\left\[e^{at}\\right\]${"altText":"(d)\/(dt)\[e^(at)\]"} for constant $a${"altText":"a"}?  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0ADC47AD] ## Logarithms
%[text] A logarithm is the inverse operation to exponentiation. As noted above, calculus identifies a special role for an exponent with base $e${"altText":"e"}, so there is a corresponding role for the natural logarithm $\\ln(t) = \\log\_e(t)${"altText":"ln(t) = log\_e(t)"}. There is one additional complication with logarithms because the domain of a logarithm is $(0,\\infty)${"altText":"(0,infinity)"}. 
%[text] ![](text:image:1224) **Activity**. Predict the type of function that will be the derivative of a natural logarithm and then run the code to generate a graph of the approximate derivative of $\\ln(t)${"altText":"ln(t)"}.
h = 0.1;            % User-defined step size h, default 0.1 %[control:slider:28de]{"position":[5,8]}
    % Run this section %[control:button:8de3]{"position":[1,2]}
if ~exist("logTHandle","var") || ~isvalid(logTHandle)
    figure                                  % Initialize a figure
    [logsHandle,logmptHandle,logTHandle] = GraphApproxDer(@(t)log(t),"ln",h,[0 8]);
    % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
else
    UpdateDerApprox(@(t)log(t),"ln",h,[0 8],logsHandle,logmptHandle,logTHandle)
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_53F21E4A] ![](text:image:8da4) **Try** Set the step size to various values and predict the function that is the derivative of $\\ln(t)${"altText":"ln(t)"}. 
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3461) **Exercise 5**. Consider the graph of the approximate value of the derivative $\\frac{d}{dt}\\left\[\\ln(t)\\right\]${"altText":"(d)\/(dt)\[ln(t)\]"}.
%[text] 1. Could the derivative be a polynomial function? Why or why not? \
poly = "no";   % User-defined answer to Exercise 5.1 %[control:dropdown:3b5c]{"position":[8,12]}
% Interact with the user based on their choices
if poly == "yes"
    disp("All polynomials p(t) diverge towards positive or negative infinity as p goes to infinity.")
    disp("That is not true of this derivative. It is not a polynomial.")
elseif poly == "no"
    disp("That is correct.")
    disp("This cannot be a polynomial because it does not have the correct limiting behavior.")
end
%%
%[text]        2\. Does the derivative have any asymptotes? Why or why not? If there are any asymptotes, what are they?
asymptotes = "yHoriz"; % User-defined answer to Exercise 5.2 %[control:dropdown:1a7e]{"position":[14,22]}
% Interact with the user based on their choices
if asymptotes == "yHoriz"
    disp("Yes, there is a horizontal asymptote.")
    disp("Adjust the horizontal asymptote to the correct value:")
    yasymp = 0;     % As the user updates this value, the section will run again %[control:editfield:85e6]{"position":[14,15]}
    xasymp = 3;     % As the user updates this value, the section will run again %[control:editfield:02c1]{"position":[14,15]}
    GraphApproxDer(@(t)log(t),"ln",h,[0 8]);     % GraphApproxDer is defined in Helper Functions
    % GraphApproxDer plots a function and calculates and plots its numerical
    % derivative with step size h
    hold on
    plot([0 8],[yasymp yasymp],"--",SeriesIndex=1);  % Plot the proposed horizontal asymptote for reference
    % Set up the format of the title string
    formatTitle = "Approximating the derivative of $y = \\%s(t)$ with step size $h=%.2f$";
    if ~exist("h","var")
        h = 0.1;                       % If necessary, set the default value for the step size
    end
    str = compose(formatTitle,"ln",h); % Include the name of the function and the step size
    title(str,"Asymptote $y=$" + yasymp,"Interpreter","latex")
    hold off
    if yasymp == 0
        disp("That is correct, the horizontal asymptote is at y=0.")
    end
elseif asymptotes == "yVert"
    disp("No, there is not a vertical asymptote. Please try again.")
elseif asymptotes == "no"
    disp("Yes, there is an asymptote. What type is it?")
end
%%
%[text]         3\. Is the derivative periodic? Why or why not? If it is periodic, what is the period and what is the amplitude?
periodic = 0;   % User-defined answer to Exercise 5.3 %[control:dropdown:940d]{"position":[12,13]}
% Interact with the user based on their choices
period = "2*pi";          amplitude = "1"; %[control:editfield:39d8]{"position":[10,16]} %[control:editfield:36ea]{"position":[39,42]}
if periodic == 1
    disp("No, this derivative is not periodic. Please try again.")
elseif periodic == 0
    disp("That is correct. This derivative is not periodic.")
end
